  "query_preview": "..(This Evening Through Thursday)\nIssued at 208 PM MDT Thu Oct 1 2026\n\nCurrent Water Vapor and upper air models show northwest flow \naloft, with an upper low located over the southern plains. 20Z \nsurface analysis shows high pressure over eastern MT, while the\ncold front that passed through us this morning is now in eastern\nNebraska. Current radar shows a line of light showers from\nnorthwestern into central SD. Temperatures sit in the 50s to mid\n60s, with generally light winds. \n\nLittle change to the overall pattern is expected through the \nweek, with zonal to northwest flow aloft hanging around. Ongoing \nlight precipitation will taper off this evening, and dry\nconditions are expected tonight through Friday. Upper ridge\nbuilds over the western CONUS Friday, resulting in warmer\ntemperatures, with highs generally in the 70s over the weekend.  \nNext disturbance comes Saturday as a wave rides the northwest flow\nthrough the Dakotas. Current models keep the better forcing in \nnorthwestern SD up into North Dakota. Very limited moisture in the\nregion, so any precipitation will likely only amount to a trace \nor a few hundredths.\n\nUpper ridge continues to dominate the western CONUS through much\nof next week, resulting in temperatures staying above normal, with\na few chances for light precipitation as weak shortwaves ride the\nnorthwest flow into the northern plains.",
